WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
1. Prepare your files and access
Open Pilot Tutorials → Upload a dataset → download the native initial-condition packet. Extract it.

Have an approved login, connected AWS resources and configured MATLAB/Connector. Choose Cloud → Advanced → ISSM.
Scripts: native-data-issm-mat/ (all four files). Data: datasets/issm-shelf-initial.mat. Prerequisites are linked on the tutorial page.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:27.000
2. Create an owned example
In Editor, enter a new name and choose New example.

This recording uses pilot-issm-mat-1119. A new ISSM example can include a seeded runme.m that must be replaced with your real script.
A seeded runme.m must be replaced by the supplied MAT run script before launch.

3
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:35.000
2. Confirm the owned workspace
The blue confirmation says the workspace example was created.

Keep this owned example selected while uploading and referencing the input. Source files and datasets use different controls.
This is an editable workspace example, separate from the read-only application examples.

4
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:53.874
3. Upload the MAT file
Scroll below Environment & resources in the left column to Datasets. Choose issm-shelf-initial.mat, then choose Upload. The selected-file count changes from 1 to 0 after upload.

This is a synthetic 5×9 gridded field file for a 10 km square shelf.
Upload stores the file. The script and schema-specific reader must still load it.

5
00:00:53.874 --> 00:01:10.623
4. Reference the staged filename
Select issm-shelf-initial.mat and choose Reference in example. Keep the baseline filename.

The confirmation gives data/issm-shelf-initial.mat. The run script must read that exact relative path.
Do not add data/ twice. Referencing makes the bytes available; it does not start the model.

6
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:27.623
5. Import all four source files
Choose runme.m, Launch.par, validate_launch_dataset.m and load_initial_conditions.m together.

Check Replace existing script files for the seeded runme.m, then choose Import script files.
The actual continuous take shows selected count 4, replacement enabled, the import action and then count 0.

7
00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:39.623
6. Check the saved-file confirmation
The confirmation names all four imported files and says Files are saved.

The imported runme.m is now the actual MAT reader/ISSM workflow. All four script hashes were verified before launch and in the submitted snapshot.
All four filenames must appear in the saved-file confirmation before you continue.

8
00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:48.623
6. Confirm the entry script
In Experiment, confirm Action = Run and Run target = runme.m.

Importing source files does not select a different target or submit an AWS job.
The four saved scripts and the referenced MAT file must all be present before review.

9
00:01:48.623 --> 00:02:00.623
7. Review the MAT experiment
Choose Review & Launch. Confirm model ISSM and example pilot-issm-mat-1119, readiness and resources.

Scroll to the bottom of the review and choose Launch cloud run explicitly.
Check 2 vCPU / 8 GiB and the existing license/Connector. Measured execution times appear in the completion card.

10
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:10.623
7. Watch the new submission
Confirm Launch cloud run at the bottom of the review.

Watch the MAT experiment progress through Staging/Submitting, then wait for Queued, Running and Completed.
The manifest links the actual saved source/data, pinned image, job and retained archive.

11
00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:15.623
8a. Initial thickness
Initial thickness is 300–500 m.

The axes span a 10 km square in metres.
Saved native PNG, zoomed to the thickness panel.

12
00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:20.623
8b. Initial surface
Surface elevation is in metres.

This floating shelf uses consistent thickness, base and surface.
Saved native PNG, zoomed to the surface panel.

13
00:02:20.623 --> 00:02:25.623
8c. Initial speed
Initial speed is in metres/year.

The next diagnostic solve computes the native velocity field.
Saved native PNG, zoomed to the initial-speed panel.

14
00:02:25.623 --> 00:02:39.623
9. Verify initialization and completion
Initialized thickness, base, surface, vx and vy match the uploaded file at all 13 native vertices, with maximum error 0.

One diagnostic SSA Stressbalance solve completed; no physical time advances.
Startup 220.723 s · AWS task 51.018 s · script 9.568328 s, including setup, mesh, initial plotting and solving.

15
00:02:39.623 --> 00:02:53.623
10. Render the current velocity field
Open Workspace → Results for the completed MAT run. Confirm StressbalanceSolution and Vel, then choose Render.

The enabled button changes while the retained native velocity field is rendered.
This Results identity belongs to the accepted MAT run; the final native field contains 13 vertices and 16 triangles.

16
00:02:53.623 --> 00:03:09.623
11. Inspect final native velocity
This is the unchanged native PNG from the accepted archive, with full axes and colour bar.

Speed is 100–180 m/year, and Vel agrees with hypot(Vx,Vy). The initial and solved velocities need not match in the interior.
The mesh spans 10 km × 10 km. This diagnostic run has no transient time axis.

17
00:03:09.623 --> 00:03:25.623
12. Interpret the checks precisely
Arithmetic-node mean thickness is 402.307692 m. Node-RMS speed sqrt(mean(Vel²)) is 142.564238 m/year; convergence count is the scalar 12.

These metrics are not area-weighted.
Five inferred non-front boundary vertices match input vx/vy. Saved spc arrays remain opaque; this boundary check is an inference, not direct decoding.

18
00:03:25.623 --> 00:03:39.623
13. Prepare the archive download
Choose Download results. When the fresh HTTPS link appears, use it to save this run’s archive.

The next step follows that link; the archive contains the native fields, figures, initialized arrays and provenance.
Download preparation uses the completed run’s retained results.

19
00:03:39.623 --> 00:03:54.623
14. Save and check your archive
Click the fresh download link. The browser saved a 15-member ZIP with valid CRC.

Every file is byte-identical to the originally checked MAT archive. Keep the archive with its input, image and output hashes.
One native execution; this cached download does not rerun the solver. Repeatability and accuracy require separate checks.

